<!-- Deep Dive: Technical Advantages -->
<section class="section" aria-label="Technical Deep Dives">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <div class="section-label-pill">Under the Hood</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">A dictation tool, and a voice assistant</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">The two projects are aimed at slightly different jobs, and the requirements each one asks of your Mac reflect that.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="deep-dive-grid">
      <div class="deep-dive-card reveal">
        <div class="deep-dive-icon">🖥️</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-title">Why the hardware bar is different</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-body">
          <p>Juno's stated 24GB RAM and M2-or-newer requirement is not an arbitrary line. Its pipeline runs cleanup, rewriting, and voice-command interpretation for app actions, all locally, which is a heavier local workload than transcription and cleanup alone.</p>
          <p>EnviousWispr is built around one job: turning your speech into clean, finished text wherever your cursor is. That narrower scope is why it runs comfortably on an M1 with no published RAM floor. Neither approach is wrong; they are optimized for different things.</p>
          <div class="deep-dive-detail">
            <strong>Why it matters:</strong> If your Mac does not meet Juno's stated bar, or you specifically want a tool that does one job well rather than several, that points toward EnviousWispr.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="deep-dive-card reveal">
        <div class="deep-dive-icon">🎙️</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-title">Dictation vs a voice layer for your Mac</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-body">
          <p>Juno describes itself as a voice layer for writing, rewriting, and taking actions. Say "Hey Juno" during a session and it can create a Note, set a Reminder, or add a Calendar entry, on top of cleaning up ordinary dictation.</p>
          <p>EnviousWispr does not create Notes, Reminders, or Calendar entries. It dictates: speech becomes text, cleaned up and pasted where you were working, every time, the same way. If you want a broader assistant, Juno covers ground we do not. If you want a dictation tool and nothing more to learn, that is what EnviousWispr is built to be.</p>
          <div class="deep-dive-detail">
            <strong>How it works:</strong> Deterministic filler and number cleanup runs first and always. AI polish (EG-1, Apple Intelligence, Ollama, or your own cloud key) runs after, with output-length validation to catch fabrication. If any step fails, you still get the last successful text, never nothing.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

